WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.480
Saying that most of our ocean floor is unmapped&nbsp;
isn't entirely true. We do have maps created by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:11.280
satellites that do all this fancy measuring&nbsp;
of differences in gravity caused by the ocean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:16.620
floor's topography. *explosion sounds* But the&nbsp;
resolution of these maps isn't great. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:21.720
satellite maps only show features larger than&nbsp;
five kilometers, which is like huge. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:27.540
our maps of friggin’ Mars have a resolution of&nbsp;
like six meters. So what's the benefit of having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:33.060
a detailed map of our ocean? For starters, it's&nbsp;
just freakin’ cool. But also the ocean is hugely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:38.040
important to our survival. Aside from providing&nbsp;
me with delicious seafood, it's also the largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:42.420
carbon sink that we have on our planet. And it&nbsp;
helps regulate all sorts of different things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:47.280
Like, I don't know, global temperatures might be&nbsp;
important. Not to mention we'll probably find new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.700
resources that we can use, which actually&nbsp;
now that I think about it could totally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:55.380
backfire because humans kind of suck at finding&nbsp;
resources and not totally exploiting them beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:59.820
repair. That being said, how do we even begin to&nbsp;
protect something that we don't even know exists?

